> API and interpretation

The sign convention is the positive-exponent unitary DFT; the inverse uses the
negative sign. Exact equally spaced peaks are a property of a compatible
periodic comb, not every truncated/nondividing support. Offset changes can be
invisible to the probability histogram while remaining visible in complex
phases. The abstract circuit has quadratic gate count, but each simulated gate
touches an exponentially large vector. Actual history stores complete post-gate
states and is capped independently of ordinary basis storage.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

